"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[9407],{2347(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Dynamic Programming","permalink":"/concepts/tags/dynamic-programming","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":5,"items":[{"id":"dynamic-programming/classic-problems","title":"Dynamic Programming: Classic Problems","description":"This lesson applies the design steps of dynamic programming to several classic problems: counting combinations, the 0/1 knapsack problem, the change with any coin system, the cheapest travel on a river, and two problems on strings, the longest common subsequence and the edit distance. The implementations of the course examples are in the course repository.","permalink":"/concepts/dynamic-programming/classic-problems"},{"id":"dynamic-programming/exercises","title":"Exercises: Dynamic Programming","description":"These exercises review the two lessons on dynamic programming. For each problem, follow the design steps of the first lesson the solutions are grouped at the end of the page.","permalink":"/concepts/dynamic-programming/exercises"},{"id":"dynamic-programming/proofs","title":"Proofs: Dynamic Programming","description":"A dynamic programming algorithm is correct if its recurrence is correct: the optimal value of each subproblem must be obtained from the optimal values of smaller subproblems. This page proves the recurrences used in the lessons.","permalink":"/concepts/dynamic-programming/proofs"},{"id":"dynamic-programming/summary","title":"Summary: Dynamic Programming","description":"Everything on one page, to review the chapter before moving on.","permalink":"/concepts/dynamic-programming/summary"},{"id":"dynamic-programming/dynamic-programming-method","title":"The Dynamic Programming Method","description":"Divide and conquer breaks a problem into subproblems and combines their solutions. It works beautifully when the subproblems are independent. But we saw in the divide and conquer chapter that the naive recursive Fibonacci solves the same subproblems again and again, and becomes exponential. Dynamic programming keeps the idea of solving a problem through its subproblems, but solves each subproblem only once, storing its solution in a table for later use.","permalink":"/concepts/dynamic-programming/dynamic-programming-method"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);